WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:04.984 --> 00:00:07.664
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:07.664 --> 00:00:09.584
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:09.584 --> 00:00:12.464
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.484 --> 00:00:14.274
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.274 --> 00:00:16.874
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:16.894 --> 00:00:18.924
things about the world at the same

00:00:18.924 --> 00:00:20.694
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.144 --> 00:00:24.244
I'm Alastair Budge, and today is

00:00:24.244 --> 00:00:26.334
the second part of our three-part

00:00:26.334 --> 00:00:29.424
mini-series on unusual British traditions.

00:00:29.824 --> 00:00:32.504
In part one, we looked at Punch and

00:00:32.514 --> 00:00:35.124
Judy, the strange puppet show that

00:00:35.124 --> 00:00:37.034
became one of the most recognisable

00:00:37.044 --> 00:00:39.024
traditions of the British seaside.

00:00:39.384 --> 00:00:41.904
In part three, next up, we'll be

00:00:41.904 --> 00:00:44.184
zooming in on the seaside itself,

00:00:44.434 --> 00:00:46.884
and talking about the rise and fall

00:00:47.084 --> 00:00:48.944
of the British seaside holiday.

00:00:49.224 --> 00:00:51.504
But in today's episode, part

00:00:51.504 --> 00:00:53.304
two, we are talking about

00:00:53.304 --> 00:00:55.144
something completely different.

00:00:55.414 --> 00:00:56.964
It's more violent, more

00:00:56.964 --> 00:00:59.724
controversial, and also illegal.

00:01:00.154 --> 00:01:01.974
And that is fox hunting.

00:01:02.774 --> 00:01:05.054
This is not just a story about

00:01:05.054 --> 00:01:07.314
whether British people like foxes.

00:01:07.634 --> 00:01:10.644
It is a story about social class, the

00:01:10.654 --> 00:01:14.144
city vs the countryside and where to draw

00:01:14.144 --> 00:01:16.504
the line between preserving tradition

00:01:16.834 --> 00:01:19.574
and tolerating unnecessary cruelty.

00:01:20.184 --> 00:01:22.234
So, let's not waste a minute

00:01:22.574 --> 00:01:24.434
and get right into it.

00:01:26.004 --> 00:01:29.984
In 1981, Disney released a film

00:01:30.184 --> 00:01:32.494
called the Fox and the Hound.

00:01:33.264 --> 00:01:35.974
It told the story of an unlikely

00:01:35.984 --> 00:01:38.934
friendship between a fox and a dog,

00:01:39.464 --> 00:01:41.264
following them from when they were

00:01:41.264 --> 00:01:43.104
young puppies playing together,

00:01:43.564 --> 00:01:45.674
to the realisation that they were

00:01:45.684 --> 00:01:48.084
being brought up to be enemies.

00:01:48.864 --> 00:01:51.314
It was the most expensive animated

00:01:51.314 --> 00:01:53.573
film ever made at that point,

00:01:53.994 --> 00:01:55.824
and, as it was a Disney film

00:01:55.824 --> 00:01:58.384
for kids, everything ended well.

00:01:58.924 --> 00:02:01.594
Nobody really gets hurt, and

00:02:01.644 --> 00:02:04.374
everyone lives happily ever after.

00:02:05.424 --> 00:02:08.194
Forty-five years later, another

00:02:08.214 --> 00:02:10.774
fox-and-hound story is still

00:02:10.804 --> 00:02:13.264
played out in dozens of villages

00:02:13.474 --> 00:02:15.194
across the English countryside.

00:02:15.644 --> 00:02:18.934
But this version is much darker.

00:02:19.824 --> 00:02:22.074
It starts happily enough, with a

00:02:22.114 --> 00:02:24.864
crowd of people gathered in a field

00:02:24.994 --> 00:02:28.244
or outside a pub, most on horseback.

00:02:29.174 --> 00:02:32.344
They wear black or red coats, white

00:02:32.384 --> 00:02:35.014
breeches, and polished black boots.

00:02:35.584 --> 00:02:38.374
The smell of horses is in the air.

00:02:38.784 --> 00:02:40.664
There is a pack of forty or

00:02:40.664 --> 00:02:42.684
fifty dogs milling around.

00:02:43.154 --> 00:02:46.533
A glass of port, strong, fortified wine,

00:02:46.593 --> 00:02:49.744
is handed up to each rider in the saddle.

00:02:50.564 --> 00:02:53.850
The steam of breath on cold winter air.

00:02:54.274 --> 00:02:56.804
A horn sounds, and the humans,

00:02:57.134 --> 00:03:00.004
dogs and horses thunder off

00:03:00.274 --> 00:03:01.904
across the muddy fields.

00:03:03.054 --> 00:03:04.634
You might think it all sounds

00:03:04.674 --> 00:03:06.374
perfectly harmless so far.

00:03:07.694 --> 00:03:10.394
What happens next is not so harmless.

00:03:11.324 --> 00:03:13.884
The dogs, all being well, will

00:03:13.924 --> 00:03:16.044
pick up the scent of a fox.

00:03:16.474 --> 00:03:18.664
They will relentlessly follow the scent,

00:03:19.074 --> 00:03:22.294
chasing the fox across fields, ditches

00:03:22.494 --> 00:03:25.494
and woods, until it cannot run any more.

00:03:26.364 --> 00:03:28.924
The pack of dogs will then pounce

00:03:29.024 --> 00:03:31.554
on the fox, ripping it to pieces.

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:34.248
It is no fairytale ending.

00:03:34.878 --> 00:03:37.648
It is also illegal, and has

00:03:37.648 --> 00:03:40.028
been for more than twenty years.

00:03:40.868 --> 00:03:44.138
So, why is it still tacitly allowed,

00:03:44.468 --> 00:03:47.028
where does it all come from, and why

00:03:47.038 --> 00:03:49.508
do some people resist the government's

00:03:49.508 --> 00:03:52.008
attempts to restrict it so fiercely?

00:03:53.148 --> 00:03:55.098
To answer those questions, we have

00:03:55.098 --> 00:03:57.318
to go back several hundred years.

00:03:58.048 --> 00:04:00.458
Now, foxes have always

00:04:00.458 --> 00:04:02.758
been a nuisance to farmers.

00:04:03.298 --> 00:04:04.988
They sneak in during the

00:04:04.988 --> 00:04:07.048
night and kill their chickens.

00:04:07.248 --> 00:04:09.548
They kill lambs in the lambing season.

00:04:10.018 --> 00:04:12.088
They are clever, hard to

00:04:12.088 --> 00:04:14.698
trap, and prolific, in other

00:04:14.698 --> 00:04:16.588
words, there are lots of them.

00:04:17.458 --> 00:04:20.148
So, for as long as people have been

00:04:20.148 --> 00:04:22.208
keeping livestock in the British

00:04:22.208 --> 00:04:24.668
Isles, they have been killing foxes.

00:04:25.538 --> 00:04:28.288
This killing was, for the most part,

00:04:28.648 --> 00:04:31.917
practical; farmers, gamekeepers,

00:04:32.058 --> 00:04:34.598
and shepherds did it with traps,

00:04:34.628 --> 00:04:37.068
with poison, and with packs of dogs.

00:04:37.388 --> 00:04:40.318
It was pest control, not "sport".

00:04:41.498 --> 00:04:44.548
And in any case, for most of the

00:04:44.558 --> 00:04:46.798
medieval and early modern period,

00:04:47.198 --> 00:04:50.298
hunting for sport, for enjoyment,

00:04:50.578 --> 00:04:53.058
was the preserve of the aristocracy,

00:04:53.348 --> 00:04:55.878
those at the top of society.

00:04:56.968 --> 00:05:00.078
And the beast you hunted was deer.

00:05:01.018 --> 00:05:04.408
Kings, dukes, and aristocrats kept

00:05:04.448 --> 00:05:07.348
enormous private deer parks, and

00:05:07.631 --> 00:05:10.258
to hunt a stag, the male deer,

00:05:10.658 --> 00:05:12.838
was the privilege of the elite.

00:05:14.088 --> 00:05:17.468
The fox, by comparison, was vermin.

00:05:18.108 --> 00:05:21.118
Hunting a lowly fox was something a farmer

00:05:21.118 --> 00:05:23.448
might do to protect his sheep or chickens.

00:05:24.008 --> 00:05:25.828
Hunting a majestic stag

00:05:26.298 --> 00:05:28.238
was something a king did.

00:05:29.483 --> 00:05:32.263
This all started to change in the

00:05:32.273 --> 00:05:34.303
seventeenth and eighteenth centuries,

00:05:34.533 --> 00:05:37.543
and it changed because of two things.

00:05:38.563 --> 00:05:40.523
The first was that the deer

00:05:40.523 --> 00:05:42.753
population started to collapse.

00:05:43.763 --> 00:05:46.353
Centuries of hunting meant that by

00:05:46.533 --> 00:05:49.843
1700 or so, the great deer herds of

00:05:49.843 --> 00:05:52.673
medieval England were largely gone; the

00:05:52.683 --> 00:05:54.803
aristocrats had killed most of them.

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:57.360
The second change was

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:58.870
the landscape itself.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.920
England used to be full of

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:04.330
forests and woods, which provided

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:06.040
the natural habitat for deer.

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:10.700
Much of this had been cut down to make

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:13.540
way for agricultural land, for fields.

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:17.150
And then in the seventeenth and eighteenth

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:19.290
centuries, there was something called

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:22.950
enclosure, and this transformed the

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:25.490
English countryside one step further.

00:06:26.413 --> 00:06:28.253
What this meant was that these

00:06:28.283 --> 00:06:31.533
big open fields were divided into

00:06:31.533 --> 00:06:34.363
smaller patches of farmland, divided

00:06:34.503 --> 00:06:36.763
by neat hedgerows and ditches.

00:06:38.053 --> 00:06:41.933
This just so happened to be almost perfect

00:06:41.993 --> 00:06:44.843
terrain for a different kind of hunt.

00:06:45.363 --> 00:06:48.093
A hunt that involved galloping flat-out

00:06:48.163 --> 00:06:50.763
across country, jumping over hedges and

00:06:50.763 --> 00:06:53.513
streams, and following a fast-running

00:06:53.563 --> 00:06:56.703
animal across miles of open ground.

00:06:57.973 --> 00:07:00.793
And that animal was the fox.

00:07:02.283 --> 00:07:03.853
There was one more thing in

00:07:03.853 --> 00:07:06.213
the fox's favour, at least from

00:07:06.213 --> 00:07:07.333
the hunter's point of view.

00:07:08.823 --> 00:07:12.863
A female deer, or a doe, typically has

00:07:12.863 --> 00:07:15.363
between one and three babies per year.

00:07:16.183 --> 00:07:18.993
A female fox, or a vixen, will

00:07:19.033 --> 00:07:21.623
typically produce four or five

00:07:21.623 --> 00:07:24.033
babies, or cubs, every spring.

00:07:24.693 --> 00:07:26.633
And in some cases, many

00:07:26.633 --> 00:07:28.723
more, sometimes ten or more.

00:07:29.843 --> 00:07:32.513
What this meant was that a hunt could

00:07:32.513 --> 00:07:35.183
ride out every Saturday of the season,

00:07:35.573 --> 00:07:38.693
kill a fox each time, and the fox

00:07:38.723 --> 00:07:41.083
population would scarcely notice.

00:07:42.346 --> 00:07:44.936
To the huntsman, this made the

00:07:44.936 --> 00:07:48.616
fox both a worthy adversary and

00:07:48.916 --> 00:07:51.836
effectively unlimited in supply.

00:07:52.961 --> 00:07:55.571
Now, fox hunting in its modern

00:07:55.581 --> 00:07:58.441
form is traditionally credited

00:07:58.481 --> 00:08:01.002
to one man: a Leicestershire

00:08:01.002 --> 00:08:03.571
landowner named Hugo Meynell.

00:08:04.991 --> 00:08:08.761
In 1753, at the age of eighteen, Meynell

00:08:08.831 --> 00:08:11.701
took over a pack of hounds at Quorndon

00:08:11.701 --> 00:08:14.961
Hall, and over the next half-century

00:08:15.361 --> 00:08:18.271
he supposedly transformed fox hunting.

00:08:18.991 --> 00:08:22.141
He bred faster, more athletic hounds.

00:08:22.461 --> 00:08:24.481
He trained them to follow

00:08:24.511 --> 00:08:27.311
the scent of a fox at speed.

00:08:27.661 --> 00:08:30.831
He pioneered the kind of long, hard,

00:08:30.901 --> 00:08:33.141
cross-country chase that became the

00:08:33.151 --> 00:08:35.571
model for every hunt that followed.

00:08:36.431 --> 00:08:39.371
Now, some modern historians have pushed

00:08:39.381 --> 00:08:41.741
back a little on this story, saying

00:08:41.741 --> 00:08:44.291
that the neat tale of Meynell as the

00:08:44.291 --> 00:08:46.231
lone genius who invented fox hunting

00:08:46.641 --> 00:08:48.401
is probably a little exaggerated.

00:08:49.171 --> 00:08:51.741
Whether it was Meynell alone, or

00:08:51.741 --> 00:08:53.441
whether he simply became the face of

00:08:53.441 --> 00:08:55.461
something that was already happening

00:08:55.531 --> 00:08:58.051
all over the country, it doesn't really

00:08:58.051 --> 00:09:00.491
matter for the purposes of our story.

00:09:01.621 --> 00:09:03.351
The important thing to understand

00:09:03.651 --> 00:09:07.011
is that by the early 1800s the

00:09:07.021 --> 00:09:09.351
basic shape of the thing we now

00:09:09.361 --> 00:09:11.821
call fox hunting was in place.

00:09:12.984 --> 00:09:16.124
And by the time we get to the Victorian

00:09:16.154 --> 00:09:18.934
era, the hunt had become something

00:09:18.944 --> 00:09:21.274
much more than a country pursuit.

00:09:21.864 --> 00:09:24.654
It had become an institution.

00:09:26.114 --> 00:09:28.924
Each hunt had a defined territory,

00:09:29.284 --> 00:09:31.874
sometimes covering several counties.

00:09:32.594 --> 00:09:35.449
Each had a Master of Foxhounds,

00:09:35.631 --> 00:09:37.264
who was the senior figure,

00:09:37.724 --> 00:09:40.354
often a wealthy local landowner.

00:09:41.114 --> 00:09:43.904
Each haa d professional staff in

00:09:43.904 --> 00:09:45.934
charge of the dogs and horses.

00:09:46.714 --> 00:09:49.284
The hunt had its own kennels, its

00:09:49.414 --> 00:09:51.864
own stables, its own subscription

00:09:51.864 --> 00:09:54.674
system, even its own dinner club.

00:09:55.984 --> 00:09:58.034
It also had its own clothing.

00:09:58.974 --> 00:10:02.724
Senior figures wore a scarlet coat, which,

00:10:02.994 --> 00:10:05.174
for reasons no one can quite agree on,

00:10:05.494 --> 00:10:08.124
is traditionally called "hunting pink".

00:10:09.234 --> 00:10:12.124
Most riders wore black or navy.

00:10:13.234 --> 00:10:16.494
Fox hunting also had its own vocabulary.

00:10:17.224 --> 00:10:20.574
Foxes were not called male and female.

00:10:20.764 --> 00:10:23.761
They were dog foxes and vixens.

00:10:24.084 --> 00:10:26.714
The fox's tail was a brush.

00:10:28.144 --> 00:10:29.504
The animals chasing the

00:10:29.504 --> 00:10:31.574
fox were never called dogs.

00:10:31.794 --> 00:10:33.914
They were a pack of hounds,

00:10:33.974 --> 00:10:35.954
with names and ranks.

00:10:36.774 --> 00:10:38.184
The huntsman did not

00:10:38.224 --> 00:10:39.434
call them with a whistle.

00:10:39.814 --> 00:10:41.524
He called them with a horn

00:10:41.904 --> 00:10:43.554
and with hunting cries like

00:10:43.674 --> 00:10:46.064
"tally-ho" and "gone away".

00:10:47.514 --> 00:10:50.404
The hunt also had its own ritual.

00:10:51.074 --> 00:10:52.834
The meet, where the hunt gathered,

00:10:53.064 --> 00:10:55.054
which was usually in front of a

00:10:55.064 --> 00:10:57.234
village pub or a country house.

00:10:57.634 --> 00:11:00.454
The stirrup cup, which is the small

00:11:00.454 --> 00:11:03.474
glass of port or cherry brandy passed

00:11:03.474 --> 00:11:05.574
up to each rider in the saddle.

00:11:06.104 --> 00:11:08.124
The blessing of the hounds.

00:11:08.304 --> 00:11:09.734
The blowing of the horn.

00:11:10.044 --> 00:11:12.104
The first cast across a field,

00:11:12.274 --> 00:11:14.234
meaning the first time the hounds

00:11:14.234 --> 00:11:16.494
were sent out across a field to

00:11:16.494 --> 00:11:18.294
try to catch the scent of a fox.

00:11:19.254 --> 00:11:21.784
And it could be a long day too.

00:11:22.474 --> 00:11:24.814
A day's hunting, from start to

00:11:24.814 --> 00:11:27.594
finish, could last six or seven hours.

00:11:28.174 --> 00:11:30.174
The hounds would be sent out across a

00:11:30.174 --> 00:11:32.714
field, picking up the scent of a fox.

00:11:33.154 --> 00:11:35.014
The hunt would gallop after

00:11:35.014 --> 00:11:37.404
them, often for miles, over

00:11:37.434 --> 00:11:39.594
hedges and ditches and walls.

00:11:40.034 --> 00:11:41.724
Riders would fall.

00:11:41.944 --> 00:11:43.704
Horses would go lame.

00:11:44.174 --> 00:11:47.144
A fox might be killed at the end of it, or

00:11:47.184 --> 00:11:49.934
might escape into a wood or down a drain.

00:11:51.114 --> 00:11:53.894
Then the hunt would move on to a new

00:11:53.934 --> 00:11:56.104
patch of country and start again.

00:11:56.994 --> 00:11:59.104
By the end of the afternoon the

00:11:59.104 --> 00:12:02.374
riders would be cold, tired and muddy.

00:12:02.884 --> 00:12:05.344
They would get off the horses, perhaps

00:12:05.594 --> 00:12:08.464
gather back at the pub, eat, drink,

00:12:08.724 --> 00:12:10.634
and tell stories about the day.

00:12:12.084 --> 00:12:14.294
And hunting was not an activity

00:12:14.294 --> 00:12:17.294
for everyone; fox hunting was

00:12:17.374 --> 00:12:19.634
overwhelmingly an upper-class

00:12:19.634 --> 00:12:21.944
and rural-middle-class pursuit.

00:12:22.504 --> 00:12:25.254
A subscription to a hunt was expensive.

00:12:25.564 --> 00:12:27.784
The horse, the kit, the time

00:12:27.804 --> 00:12:29.514
off work mid-week or on a

00:12:29.514 --> 00:12:31.964
Saturday: all of it cost money.

00:12:33.184 --> 00:12:35.364
Farmers were welcomed, because

00:12:35.554 --> 00:12:37.194
the hunt rode across their land.

00:12:37.554 --> 00:12:41.104
But the masters of foxhounds, the people

00:12:41.234 --> 00:12:44.464
who actually ran the hunts, these were

00:12:44.474 --> 00:12:47.234
almost always the people who owned the big

00:12:47.354 --> 00:12:49.784
country houses and the fields around them.

00:12:50.944 --> 00:12:53.814
Now, even back in the Victorian era,

00:12:54.234 --> 00:12:56.724
it had its fair share of detractors.

00:12:57.594 --> 00:13:01.174
In the 1890s, Oscar Wilde famously

00:13:01.184 --> 00:13:03.054
summed up the English fox hunt,

00:13:03.324 --> 00:13:06.014
calling it "the unspeakable in

00:13:06.014 --> 00:13:08.574
full pursuit of the uneatable".

00:13:09.264 --> 00:13:11.674
In other words, all these ridiculous

00:13:11.674 --> 00:13:13.444
people chasing something that you

00:13:13.444 --> 00:13:15.346
don't even eat at the end of it.

00:13:15.874 --> 00:13:17.954
The whole thing existed for

00:13:17.954 --> 00:13:19.954
its own sake; it was pointless.

00:13:21.146 --> 00:13:24.446
By the late nineteenth century, Wilde

00:13:24.456 --> 00:13:26.936
was not alone in finding it strange.

00:13:28.116 --> 00:13:29.586
A growing number of people,

00:13:29.756 --> 00:13:31.656
particularly in the cities, were

00:13:31.666 --> 00:13:33.676
beginning to think that the violent

00:13:33.686 --> 00:13:35.746
killing of an animal for enjoyment

00:13:36.026 --> 00:13:38.626
had no place in a civilised country.

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:42.290
And people started vocally opposing

00:13:42.290 --> 00:13:44.830
it, calling for it to be banned.

00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:48.170
The first serious organised

00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:51.841
opposition dates back to 1925.

00:13:52.963 --> 00:13:55.699
In that year, a group of campaigners

00:13:55.898 --> 00:13:58.428
founded the League for the Prohibition

00:13:58.428 --> 00:14:01.028
of Cruel Sports, which is today known

00:14:01.028 --> 00:14:03.558
simply as the League Against Cruel Sports.

00:14:05.008 --> 00:14:07.808
Its early efforts focused on softer

00:14:07.858 --> 00:14:10.128
popular targets: like hunting rabbits

00:14:10.128 --> 00:14:12.898
with dogs, hunting otters or stags.

00:14:14.048 --> 00:14:16.758
These efforts had some success, and

00:14:17.048 --> 00:14:19.518
laid the groundwork for the idea

00:14:19.518 --> 00:14:22.058
that fox hunting wasn't untouchable.

00:14:22.964 --> 00:14:26.248
But it would take another generation for

00:14:26.248 --> 00:14:29.758
that idea to translate into direct action.

00:14:31.218 --> 00:14:34.248
In 1958, members of the League

00:14:34.468 --> 00:14:37.578
laid false scent trails to disrupt

00:14:37.608 --> 00:14:39.878
a stag hunt in the West Country.

00:14:41.178 --> 00:14:43.648
In other words, they put fake smells

00:14:43.688 --> 00:14:45.628
that would trick and confuse the

00:14:45.628 --> 00:14:47.768
hunting dogs, committing the first

00:14:47.778 --> 00:14:50.428
modern act of "hunt sabotage".

00:14:51.838 --> 00:14:54.228
This proved to be very effective,

00:14:54.528 --> 00:14:56.988
and groups sprang up throughout the

00:14:56.988 --> 00:14:59.348
country, groups that became known

00:14:59.348 --> 00:15:02.418
as "sabs", short for saboteurs.

00:15:03.648 --> 00:15:05.378
They would arrive at hunting

00:15:05.378 --> 00:15:07.588
meets with horns, whistles,

00:15:07.798 --> 00:15:09.828
and bottles of false scent.

00:15:10.288 --> 00:15:12.448
They would try to confuse the hounds,

00:15:12.738 --> 00:15:15.408
distract the huntsmen, and steer

00:15:15.438 --> 00:15:17.988
the hunt away from any actual fox.

00:15:19.148 --> 00:15:21.868
In response, the hunts would close

00:15:21.868 --> 00:15:24.698
ranks, push the saboteurs away, and

00:15:24.718 --> 00:15:27.018
complain to the police about people

00:15:27.038 --> 00:15:29.208
trespassing on private property.

00:15:30.848 --> 00:15:35.028
On bad days, the two sides came to blows.

00:15:35.478 --> 00:15:37.068
People were injured.

00:15:37.608 --> 00:15:40.848
In the 1990s, two saboteurs were

00:15:40.848 --> 00:15:43.528
killed while trying to prevent a hunt.

00:15:44.753 --> 00:15:48.173
Meanwhile, public opinion was shifting.

00:15:49.403 --> 00:15:53.073
By the 1990s, opinion polls regularly

00:15:53.073 --> 00:15:56.083
showed that a clear majority of British

00:15:56.083 --> 00:15:59.733
people wanted hunting with hounds banned.

00:16:01.383 --> 00:16:05.303
And in 1997, Tony Blair and Labour

00:16:05.563 --> 00:16:07.983
won a landslide general election.

00:16:09.253 --> 00:16:11.283
Although it was only a small part

00:16:11.443 --> 00:16:13.232
of the manifesto,  Labour had

00:16:13.232 --> 00:16:15.842
promised to hold a vote on whether

00:16:15.852 --> 00:16:17.972
hunting with dogs should be banned.

00:16:18.430 --> 00:16:19.911
But… nothing happened.

00:16:20.611 --> 00:16:22.551
The fox hunting question sat

00:16:22.551 --> 00:16:25.011
there for the next seven years,

00:16:25.061 --> 00:16:26.591
without anything being done.

00:16:27.931 --> 00:16:30.441
In those years, the hunting debate

00:16:30.681 --> 00:16:33.161
became one of the most unusual political

00:16:33.161 --> 00:16:35.371
battles in modern British history.

00:16:36.791 --> 00:16:39.571
On the surface, it was about something

00:16:39.881 --> 00:16:42.701
that affected almost nobody's daily life.

00:16:43.161 --> 00:16:45.171
It was an incredibly niche

00:16:45.181 --> 00:16:47.831
activity, with a tiny fraction

00:16:48.041 --> 00:16:49.601
of the population doing it.

00:16:50.671 --> 00:16:53.931
And yet it produced more parliamentary

00:16:53.931 --> 00:16:56.041
debate, more political anger,

00:16:56.241 --> 00:16:58.901
and more street protest than most

00:16:58.941 --> 00:17:00.881
other domestic issues of the time.

00:17:02.112 --> 00:17:05.292
As to the question of why, well, it

00:17:05.292 --> 00:17:07.382
was partly because it had become a

00:17:07.402 --> 00:17:10.272
stand-in for a much bigger argument

00:17:10.452 --> 00:17:12.942
about rural and urban Britain.

00:17:14.312 --> 00:17:16.742
To many in the countryside, where

00:17:16.962 --> 00:17:19.102
hunting was more broadly supported,

00:17:19.552 --> 00:17:22.512
the proposed ban felt like a city-based

00:17:22.522 --> 00:17:25.432
government attacking a way of life it

00:17:25.462 --> 00:17:28.552
did not understand, and interfering in

00:17:28.552 --> 00:17:30.792
a tradition that didn't affect them.

00:17:32.022 --> 00:17:34.412
They argued that hunting was not

00:17:34.412 --> 00:17:36.962
simply a hobby for aristocrats.

00:17:37.122 --> 00:17:39.542
They said it supported rural jobs,

00:17:39.892 --> 00:17:42.922
helped manage fox numbers, kept hounds

00:17:43.062 --> 00:17:45.752
and horses in work, and formed part

00:17:45.772 --> 00:17:48.552
of the social fabric of village life.

00:17:49.832 --> 00:17:53.302
In 2002, there was a huge demonstration

00:17:53.382 --> 00:17:56.212
through London, with four hundred

00:17:56.422 --> 00:17:58.672
thousand people descending on the

00:17:58.672 --> 00:18:01.492
capital in support of hunting.

00:18:02.592 --> 00:18:04.482
At the time, it was one of the

00:18:04.482 --> 00:18:06.442
largest political demonstrations

00:18:06.742 --> 00:18:08.462
London had ever seen.

00:18:09.902 --> 00:18:11.952
And the demonstration was not

00:18:12.032 --> 00:18:13.682
really just about hunting.

00:18:14.162 --> 00:18:16.872
It was about everything that rural Britain

00:18:17.172 --> 00:18:19.642
felt the new government was getting wrong:

00:18:20.212 --> 00:18:23.222
rules on shooting, the closure of village

00:18:23.242 --> 00:18:26.552
post offices, falling farm incomes, the

00:18:26.562 --> 00:18:28.822
broad and bitter sense of being looked

00:18:28.842 --> 00:18:32.052
down on by a metropolitan political class

00:18:32.402 --> 00:18:34.652
that did not understand country life.

00:18:35.984 --> 00:18:38.704
And to many in the cities, fox

00:18:38.704 --> 00:18:41.764
hunting simply had no reason to exist.

00:18:42.134 --> 00:18:44.544
It was unnecessarily cruel,

00:18:44.894 --> 00:18:47.284
terrifying a fox into running for

00:18:47.284 --> 00:18:49.804
its life until it grew tired and was

00:18:49.814 --> 00:18:52.494
ripped to shreds by a pack of dogs.

00:18:53.084 --> 00:18:56.004
This simply shouldn't be legal in

00:18:56.004 --> 00:18:58.074
a civilised society, they said.

00:18:58.924 --> 00:19:01.564
Various bills came forward, were

00:19:01.564 --> 00:19:03.704
debated, were amended, and were

00:19:03.704 --> 00:19:05.344
sent back to be debated again.

00:19:06.034 --> 00:19:07.584
The House of Commons had

00:19:07.584 --> 00:19:09.284
a huge Labour majority.

00:19:09.704 --> 00:19:13.444
It voted again and again for a total ban.

00:19:14.384 --> 00:19:16.514
The House of Lords had many

00:19:16.544 --> 00:19:18.844
hunt sympathisers, and it

00:19:18.854 --> 00:19:21.104
blocked the bills every time.

00:19:22.004 --> 00:19:24.194
And by the way, if you'd like to learn

00:19:24.214 --> 00:19:26.724
more about what that all means, and how

00:19:26.744 --> 00:19:28.934
the British political system works, I'd

00:19:28.934 --> 00:19:32.104
recommend episode number 592, as that

00:19:32.104 --> 00:19:34.634
goes into all of that in great detail.

00:19:36.284 --> 00:19:40.084
Eventually, in 2004, the government

00:19:40.104 --> 00:19:42.614
did something it almost never does.

00:19:43.754 --> 00:19:45.884
It invoked a mechanism that

00:19:45.924 --> 00:19:47.974
allows the House of Commons to

00:19:48.074 --> 00:19:50.654
overrule the House of Lords and

00:19:50.694 --> 00:19:53.264
pass a bill without their consent.

00:19:54.154 --> 00:19:56.514
The Hunting Act was only the seventh

00:19:56.534 --> 00:19:58.664
piece of legislation in nearly a

00:19:58.684 --> 00:20:01.314
century to be passed in this way.

00:20:02.744 --> 00:20:06.604
The Act made it illegal to hunt wild

00:20:06.634 --> 00:20:08.804
mammals with dogs in England and

00:20:08.804 --> 00:20:11.554
Wales, with a small number of narrow

00:20:11.584 --> 00:20:14.144
exemptions, mostly for pest control.

00:20:15.294 --> 00:20:17.224
It came into force on the

00:20:17.297 --> 00:20:19.984
18th of February 2005.

00:20:20.774 --> 00:20:23.354
Fox hunting was, for the first

00:20:23.354 --> 00:20:25.594
time in history, illegal.

00:20:26.664 --> 00:20:29.104
But if you had thought this would have

00:20:29.104 --> 00:20:31.824
meant the hunts would have stopped, no no.

00:20:32.894 --> 00:20:34.874
The morning after the ban came into

00:20:34.874 --> 00:20:38.374
force, the hunts went out as usual.

00:20:39.094 --> 00:20:41.094
Their message was clear: the

00:20:41.094 --> 00:20:43.194
tradition would continue.

00:20:44.784 --> 00:20:46.164
Technically, what they

00:20:46.164 --> 00:20:47.594
did was something new.

00:20:47.934 --> 00:20:49.814
It was "trail hunting".

00:20:51.004 --> 00:20:53.484
A scent was laid in advance along

00:20:53.484 --> 00:20:55.234
a route across the countryside,

00:20:55.574 --> 00:20:57.844
typically the urine of a fox.

00:20:58.574 --> 00:21:00.014
The hounds were then

00:21:00.024 --> 00:21:02.224
released to follow the scent.

00:21:02.734 --> 00:21:04.864
The horses, the red coats, the

00:21:04.864 --> 00:21:06.744
horn, the meet, the stirrup cup:

00:21:07.094 --> 00:21:09.584
all of it carried on as before.

00:21:10.587 --> 00:21:13.637
In theory, the chase was now only after

00:21:13.647 --> 00:21:16.857
a trail, an artificially laid smell.

00:21:17.777 --> 00:21:20.807
And if a real fox happened to be in

00:21:20.807 --> 00:21:23.027
the way and the hounds chased it down,

00:21:23.337 --> 00:21:26.117
well, that was an unfortunate accident.

00:21:27.247 --> 00:21:29.617
Critics were not convinced.

00:21:29.947 --> 00:21:31.807
It was a ruse, they said.

00:21:32.337 --> 00:21:34.527
Trail hunting was simply fox

00:21:34.527 --> 00:21:36.377
hunting by a different name.

00:21:37.397 --> 00:21:39.627
Anti-hunting groups stepped up the

00:21:39.627 --> 00:21:42.307
pressure, monitoring hunts, filming

00:21:42.307 --> 00:21:44.857
them, and reporting incidents where they

00:21:44.857 --> 00:21:47.027
believed a real fox had been killed.

00:21:47.787 --> 00:21:50.037
Even the police were blunt about it.

00:21:50.877 --> 00:21:53.797
A senior officer described trail hunting

00:21:53.947 --> 00:21:56.017
as, and I'm quoting directly here,

00:21:56.337 --> 00:21:59.337
a "smokescreen" that "gives people

00:21:59.347 --> 00:22:01.627
the opportunity to carry on hunting,

00:22:01.897 --> 00:22:03.827
as they always said they would when

00:22:03.827 --> 00:22:05.757
the ban came in twenty years ago".

00:22:07.087 --> 00:22:10.046
There were prosecutions; people taken to

00:22:10.086 --> 00:22:13.047
court and convicted, but the punishments

00:22:13.047 --> 00:22:15.937
were typically light: a fine of a few

00:22:15.937 --> 00:22:18.377
hundred pounds or a police caution.

00:22:19.707 --> 00:22:21.727
For nearly two decades, then,

00:22:22.027 --> 00:22:23.967
the hunts, or rather the trail

00:22:23.997 --> 00:22:26.347
hunts, continued in this way.

00:22:27.497 --> 00:22:30.087
That was, until earlier this year.

00:22:31.377 --> 00:22:34.167
In March 2026, the British

00:22:34.167 --> 00:22:36.237
government announced that trail

00:22:36.237 --> 00:22:38.817
hunting itself was to be banned.

00:22:39.737 --> 00:22:41.287
It was a commitment in the

00:22:41.297 --> 00:22:42.817
Labour government's manifesto.

00:22:43.547 --> 00:22:46.797
And on the 26th of March, the Animal

00:22:46.797 --> 00:22:49.477
Welfare Minister, Baroness Sue Hayman,

00:22:49.857 --> 00:22:53.037
opened a public consultation on exactly

00:22:53.187 --> 00:22:55.517
how to bring the new ban into force.

00:22:56.677 --> 00:22:59.467
As of the time of recording, the findings

00:22:59.647 --> 00:23:02.647
haven't been published, but the loophole

00:23:02.797 --> 00:23:05.367
that has kept fox hunting alive for twenty

00:23:05.367 --> 00:23:08.537
years looks highly likely to be closed.

00:23:09.336 --> 00:23:12.607
It is bad news for the men in red coats.

00:23:12.947 --> 00:23:15.587
But if you are a fox, it might

00:23:15.617 --> 00:23:18.307
just be very good news indeed.

00:23:19.757 --> 00:23:22.217
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:23:22.237 --> 00:23:24.886
episode on fox hunting, the second

00:23:24.887 --> 00:23:26.427
of our three-part mini-series

00:23:26.447 --> 00:23:28.107
on unusual British traditions.

00:23:28.517 --> 00:23:31.127
Next up, in the third and final part of

00:23:31.127 --> 00:23:33.227
the mini-series, it'll be the rise and

00:23:33.227 --> 00:23:35.827
fall of the British seaside holiday.

00:23:36.277 --> 00:23:37.807
As always, I would love to know

00:23:37.807 --> 00:23:39.137
what you thought of this episode.

00:23:39.377 --> 00:23:41.207
What do you think about fox hunting?

00:23:41.587 --> 00:23:43.037
Are there similar traditions in

00:23:43.047 --> 00:23:45.137
your country, and what does the

00:23:45.137 --> 00:23:47.017
future have in store for them?

00:23:47.567 --> 00:23:49.197
I would love to know, so let's

00:23:49.197 --> 00:23:50.517
get this discussion started.

00:23:50.767 --> 00:23:51.867
You can head right into our

00:23:51.867 --> 00:23:53.517
community forum, which is at

00:23:53.517 --> 00:23:56.187
community.leonardoenglish.com and get

00:23:56.187 --> 00:23:58.497
chatting away to other curious minds.

00:23:59.107 --> 00:24:00.977
You've been listening to English Learning

00:24:00.977 --> 00:24:03.947
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:24:04.327 --> 00:24:07.457
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:24:07.517 --> 00:24:10.137
I'll catch you in the next episode.

